Introduction and Background
\layout ShortFoilhead

Introduction
\layout Itemize

Queueing theory dates back to the beginning of the 20th Century and the
 pioneering work of Agner Krarup Erlang (1878-1929).
\layout Itemize

Erlang was a Danish Telecoms Engineer who published the first paper on queueing
 theory 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \cite{Erlang:theory09}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

Initially, telephone call routing was achieved manually.
 
\layout Itemize

The invention of the 
\emph on 
automatic telephone exchange
\emph default 
 (through the Strowger switch) in 1891 brought about the need for a theoretical
 study of the operation of telephone exchanges.
\layout Itemize

Erlang's provided theoretical studies of these circuit switching networks.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Computer Networks
\layout Itemize

Circuit switched networks were designed for telephone communication between
 two correspondents.
 
\layout Itemize

By the early 1960s 
\emph on 
packet switching networks
\emph default 
 came into being.
\layout Itemize

ARPANET was predecessor of the internet.
