Properties III
\layout Itemize

Independence of Arrivals
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:poissonIndependence}

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard

Consider a single Poisson process, if we look within this process at two
 
\emph on 
disjoint
\emph default 
 time intervals (
\emph on 
i.e.

\emph default 
 periods of time that don't overlap).
 Assume we have 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}$
\end_inset 

 arrivals in the first interval and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}$
\end_inset 

 arrivals in the second interval.
 The distribution of arrivals in the two intervals are independent of each
 other, 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k_{1},k_{2}\right)=P\left(k_{1}\right)P\left(k_{2}\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Properties IV
\layout Itemize

Similarity of Arrival Distributions for Identical Intervals
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:poissonSimilarities}

\end_inset 


\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard

Consider a single Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, let the number of arrivals between time 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s+t$
\end_inset 

 be given by 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}$
\end_inset 

.
 Now consider a second independent Poisson process with the same rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, let the number of arrivals between time 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r+t$
\end_inset 

 be given by 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}$
\end_inset 

.
 The distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{1}$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{2}$
\end_inset 

 are then equal, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k_{1}\right)=P\left(k_{2}\right).\]

\end_inset 

 
\end_deeper 
