\layout ShortFoilhead

Little's Formula
\layout Itemize

Important quality of service issue is the average delay.
\layout Itemize

We would also like to be able to compute 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

the average number in the queue and 
\layout Itemize

the average time spent queueing.
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

We introduce Little's formula, a very general formula.
\layout Itemize

Can be applied to 
\begin_inset Formula $G/G/1$
\end_inset 

 queues.
 
\layout Itemize

Gives the relationship between 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

Steady-state average size of a queue, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

The steady-state arrival rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

The steady-state customer delay, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Time and Steady State Averages
\layout Itemize

We have already introduced the distribution means and sample based means.
 
\layout Itemize

Time based averages are a further type of average.
 
\layout Itemize

The time average is the average value of a particular function over time.
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{f}_{t}=\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}f\left(\tau\right)d\tau.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

The derivation of Little's formula relies on an assumption.
 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

Assume that averages under the steady state distribution are equivalent
 to the time average in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

, 
\layout Itemize

Average number in the system as a function of time is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}_{t}=\frac{1}{t}\int_{0}^{t}N\left(\tau\right)d\tau\]

\end_inset 

and the time average after in the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $t\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}\bar{N}\left(t\right).\]

\end_inset 

which we assume is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle k\right\rangle _{p\left(k\right)}\equiv\bar{N}$
\end_inset 

.
 See pg 156-157 in 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \cite{Bertsekas:data92}

\end_inset 

.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

The Formula
\layout Itemize

Applies under steady state conditions.
\layout Itemize

Under the steady state assumptions it states,
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{N}=\lambda\bar{T}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard


\series bold 
Example
\series default 
: Nightclub queue.
\layout Standard

You are driving past a nightclub, wondering how long you will have to wait
 to go in.
 You notice on Thursday nights that the queue typically contains 20 people
 and that people arrive at a rate of 40 per hour.
 
\layout Standard

According to Little's formula, how long would you expect to have to wait
 to get in? 
\layout Standard


\series bold 
Answer
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
answer{
\end_inset 

The waiting time is given by Little's formula: 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}=20$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=40$
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{T}=\frac{\bar{N}}{\lambda}=\frac{20}{40}=\frac{1}{2}\textrm{hour}$
\end_inset 

.
 
